# Downgrade policy: Pro to Free

What a user who cancels Pro can and can't do with what they already have once the paid period ends, and how the backend enforces it. `docs/brief.md` wins over this document wherever they disagree.

## Principles

1. **Nothing the user made is deleted or degraded because they stopped paying.** Every book, page, and photo is kept at full size and served unchanged. The only thing that removes content is the user, or the normal 14-day Trash purge they started.
2. **Over a limit, you can shrink but not grow.** You can read, download, and remove everything. Anything that would add a book or add stored bytes is refused until usage is back under the Free allowance.
3. **Coverage is derived, never stored.** No "locked" flag is written to any book. Whether a book is editable is computed from the current plan every time it is asked. When the user resubscribes, everything works again at once, and there is nothing to migrate.
4. **One gate.** The browser app, the public API, the MCP connection, and background jobs all go through the same backend check and get the same answer.

## When it applies

Cancellation takes effect at the end of the paid period. Until then the account is Pro in every respect. The billing provider owns subscription truth. The backend reads the plan from it, through a last-known mirror kept in sync by the provider's webhooks. From the moment that mirror says the subscription has ended, every check uses Free limits. Nothing runs at the boundary: no job, no sweep, no notification.

## The Free limits that bind

| Allowance | Free | What counts |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| Books | 25 | Books not in Trash |
| One uploaded photo | 40 MB | Size of the photo as uploaded |
| Total storage | 1.5 GB | All stored photo bytes, **including photos in books that are in Trash**, until Trash removes them permanently |

## The rules

### Books (aggregate count)

- **Covered books.** If the user has more than 25 books not in Trash, the **25 oldest by creation date** are covered. The rest are not. This is worked out on every request, so trashing a covered book or deleting one permanently immediately makes the next-oldest book covered.
- **A covered book** works normally, subject to the storage and photo-size rules below.
- **An uncovered book** keeps every page, photo, and its history. The user can:
  - open and view it,
  - download or export it, where the product offers that,
  - remove photos or pages from it, and move it to Trash.

  Every other action is refused: editing layout, text, or pages, adding photos, duplicating the book, and ordering prints.
- **Creating a book** is refused while 25 or more books are outside Trash.
- **Restoring a book from Trash** counts as adding a book, so it is refused while 25 or more books are outside Trash. It never grows storage, because the book's bytes were still counted while it sat in Trash.

### Total storage (aggregate bytes)

- While stored bytes are at or above 1.5 GB, **all existing content stays readable and removable**.
- **Writes that don't add bytes stay allowed** in covered books: rearranging pages and photos, editing text and layout, adding empty pages, placing a photo that is already stored, and removing anything.
- **Anything that adds bytes is refused** if it would leave usage above 1.5 GB. That means uploading photos through any route, and any other operation that stores new photo bytes. An upload is allowed only when current usage plus open upload reservations plus the new photo's size fit within 1.5 GB.
- **Storage is freed only when bytes are permanently removed.** Moving a book to Trash frees nothing. Its bytes stop counting when Trash purges it after 14 days, or earlier if the user deletes it permanently from Trash.

### One photo (per-item size)

- **Existing photos over 40 MB are kept and served unchanged.** They are not recompressed or resized, and they print at full quality in covered books.
- **New photos must be 40 MB or smaller.** This applies to every upload route.
- Placing or moving an existing oversized photo is not a new upload, so the per-photo limit doesn't apply to it. If an operation would store a new copy of the bytes, it counts as a new photo and has to meet both the 40 MB limit and the storage rule.

## Checking against the typical case

The case from the brief: 40 books, 2.6 GB stored (300 MB of it in Trash), and fifteen photos of 60–110 MB.

| | Usage | Free limit | Result |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| Books | 40 | 25 | The 25 oldest are covered. The 15 newest can be viewed, downloaded, removed, or trashed, but not edited or printed. |
| Storage | 2.6 GB | 1.5 GB | 1.1 GB over. Nothing is lost. No uploads until usage is under 1.5 GB. |
| Big photos | 15 × 60–110 MB | 40 MB each | All 15 stay at full size. New uploads are capped at 40 MB. |

The day after the period ends, this user:

- **Can** open all 40 books, download any of them, and keep editing the 25 oldest: rearranging, captioning, re-laying out, and placing photos already in their books.
- **Can't** create a book, edit the 15 newest, upload a photo of any size, or upload a photo over 40 MB even after freeing space.
- **To upload again**, they need usage below 1.5 GB minus the new photo's size. Emptying Trash takes them to 2.3 GB, which is still 0.8 GB over. They need to permanently remove at least about 0.8 GB more. Moving books to Trash does nothing until those books are purged or deleted permanently.
- **To edit a newer book or create one**, they move books to Trash or delete them until 24 or fewer books are outside Trash. Trashing 15 books makes all the remaining ones covered. Trashing 16 lets them create a book.
- **If they resubscribe** to Pro, all 40 books are editable and uploads up to 120 MB resume immediately, provided usage is within 4 GB. It is: 2.6 GB.

Pro-era limits never bind this user under Pro. 40 is at most 60, 2.6 GB is at most 4 GB, and 110 MB is at most 120 MB. So the only state change is the plan.

## How the backend enforces it

### Where the rules live

- **Plan constants.** Book count, per-photo size, and total storage for Free and Pro are constants in a shared domain package, per the brief. They never come from env or a provider dashboard.
- **Entitlement functions.** Pure functions in the same package take the plan, the account's usage, and the book list, and answer:
  - which books are covered (oldest N outside Trash),
  - whether a new book fits,
  - whether N more bytes fit,
  - whether a photo of size S is allowed.
- **Backend mutations.** Every mutation that touches books or photos calls those functions before doing anything. This is the only enforcement point, and the web app, public API, MCP tools, and background jobs all reach data through these mutations. UI checks are for display only and are never trusted.

### What each gate reads

- **Plan.** The billing-provider mirror, read on each request and never cached in the book.
- **Book count and coverage.** Books outside Trash, ordered by creation time, with ID as a tiebreaker so the order is stable.
- **Storage used.** A per-account counter in the database, covering every stored photo, including photos in trashed books. It is updated when an upload finalizes (by the measured size) and when bytes are permanently removed. Deletion paths keep it accurate on every step.

### Uploads

These follow the standard reserve-then-finalize flow, using Free limits once the plan is Free:

1. **Request.** Check the declared size against the per-photo limit. Then atomically reserve the declared size against `allowance − used − open reservations`. The reservation expires, and the signed upload URL never outlives it.
2. **Finalize.** Recheck against the current plan, so an upload started under Pro that finalizes after the period ends meets Free limits. Refuse if the measured size exceeds the declared size or the per-photo limit. On success, charge the measured size once. On refusal, release the reservation and clean up the bytes through the object-write ledger.

### Errors

Every refusal returns a product-owned error that names the limit that was hit. It never says "subscription expired" and never includes provider details:

| Code | When | Message names |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `book_not_covered` | A gated action on an uncovered book | the 25-book limit on Free |
| `book_limit_reached` | Creating or restoring a book at 25 | the 25-book limit |
| `storage_full` | An upload or byte-adding operation that wouldn't fit | the 1.5 GB limit and current usage |
| `photo_too_large` | A new photo over 40 MB | the 40 MB per-photo limit |

The surface that shows the error offers the remedies that actually clear it. Pro raises every one of these ceilings, so upgrading is an honest option, alongside trashing books, emptying Trash, or deleting photos. The API and MCP return the code and message, and the caller decides what to do.

### What the UI does and doesn't show

- **The cancellation confirmation** in billing settings states what will happen at period end, using the user's real numbers.
- **An uncovered book** opens read-only, with an inline note in the book saying why and what fixes it. Edit controls are hidden or disabled there, because the server would refuse them anyway.
- **The upload control** in an over-storage account shows `storage_full` when used, not beforehand.
- **There is no standing site-wide banner or counter.** Usage against limits lives in billing settings and at the moment a limit is hit.

## Assumptions (to confirm)

These were judgment calls made without the owner. Each one can be changed in the entitlement functions alone.

1. **Which books stay editable: oldest 25 by creation date.** This is stable and predictable, and it can't be gamed. The alternative is letting the user choose which 25. That would need a stored selection, so it isn't the default here.
2. **Uncovered books can't order prints.** Printing an uncovered book is treated as a gated action. Ordering prints is revenue and doesn't consume plan resources, so allowing it is a reasonable reversal. If the owner prefers that, it is a one-line change.
3. **Books in Trash don't count toward the 25, but their bytes do count toward storage.** The storage half matches the brief's intent that Trash is retained content. Counting trashed books toward the book limit as well would make Trash useless as a way back under 25.
4. **Users can permanently delete from Trash before day 14.** The brief only states the automatic purge. Without early deletion, freeing storage would take up to two weeks.
5. **Storage counts uploaded photo bytes only.** Thumbnails, print renders, and other derived files are not counted.
6. **Placing an existing photo again references the same stored bytes,** so it doesn't add storage or count as a new upload. Photo edits such as crops and rotations are stored as metadata, not as new files. If either is untrue, those operations become byte-adding and fall under the storage and per-photo rules.
7. **No grace period.** Free limits apply the moment the billing provider reports the period has ended. Nothing is deleted at any point, so a grace period wouldn't protect any data.
8. **Uploads in flight at the boundary meet Free limits at finalize.** This keeps one rule for one moment. Reservations are short-lived, so very few users will hit it.
9. **No code exists yet.** The package and function names above describe the intended shape, not existing modules.
